1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
If you write code with AI, you've probably heard about the term spec-driven development.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:10,960
In this video, I'm going to break down exactly what it is and how you can use it in your workflow to improve the quality of your code that you generate when working with AI agents.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:21,280
One of the big challenges I found when I was approaching this topic is there are so many different frameworks, there's a lot of different terminology, and no one really agrees on how to implement spec-driven development.

4
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,040
Everyone is doing it slightly differently.

5
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:30,480
So in this video, I'm going to give you an opinionated and much simpler breakdown of how I use spec driven development in my own workflow.

6
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,560
Okay, let's start with a quick definition.

7
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:41,680
So spec-driven development is the idea that instead of just prompting first and figuring things out as you go, you define a specification up front.

8
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:45,764
And this is just a document that describes your intent about what you want to build.

9
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:53,680
It's a short document that defines what you're building, the constraints, so what you're not building and what you're not doing, and the key decisions.

10
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:56,800
And it's like a contract that you can use for how your code should behave.

11
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:03,920
So when you tell an AI agent to go and add authentication to your application, there are so many things you need to consider when building a feature like this.

12
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:08,080
Claude is pretty good about prompting you and asking you some clarifying questions.

13
00:01:08,334 --> 00:01:12,880
But ultimately, there are so many decisions you need to make about how to implement this feature.

14
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:18,560
And if you just allow the agent to work on its own, it's going to guess about what you actually want.

15
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:22,628
It's going to make decisions that maybe don't align with how you should actually build the feature.

16
00:01:22,926 --> 00:01:26,000
So the way you fix this is to use spec-driven development.

17
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:35,556
And so what we do, instead of just telling the AI to add authentication, what we're going to do is we're going to work with the agent to write a specification, which is a plan for what we want.

18
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:38,640
We're going to define what we're building, the constraints.

19
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,200
So things like what libraries do we not want to add?

20
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:43,280
How should the feature be implemented?

21
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:44,612
Should we add tests?

22
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:46,080
How should we add tests?

23
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:47,588
What libraries should we use?

24
00:01:48,142 --> 00:01:50,720
Then the AI doesn't have to guess about what you want.

25
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:57,360
So before you might have a vague prompt like add authentication to the app, but with a spec, we can be really, really clear.

26
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:04,708
So we can say we want JWT tokens, we don't want password reset, we don't want OAuth, we don't want to install new dependencies.

27
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:09,892
What we're doing now is we're being much more clear to the AI agent about what we want and what's in scope.

28
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:13,600
So one thing I would say is that there's a lot of confusion about the terminology.

29
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:20,960
I hear words like PRD, technical design doc, specs, all of these words can be a bit confusing.

30
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:24,004
And I think people are using these terminologies very differently.

31
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:30,852
So in my mind, the way software engineering has always worked is you typically have a product requirements document.

32
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:35,200
This document is intended for human beings to read.

33
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:43,360
So this is for product managers, stakeholders to decide about what are we building, why are we building it, and what are the what's the business value for this feature?

34
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:49,476
Then in most tech companies, you'll have some kind of architecture or design doc for any significant feature.

35
00:02:49,966 --> 00:03:04,708
And this is intended for engineers, and this is all about how we're building something, thinking about the architecture, thinking about the constraints, thinking about scalability and security and all of the important things we need to consider as software engineers when we're building anything.

36
00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:09,600
And then the other document you can think about is the AI spec, which is what we're talking about today.

37
00:03:09,902 --> 00:03:13,600
And in my mind, this is a document designed specifically for AI agents.

38
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:20,804
So what we put in this document is not designed necessarily for humans, it's more designed as an execution document for agents.

39
00:03:21,454 --> 00:03:32,516
So a spec for an agent should probably include some why and how, some context about what we're building and why, because that will help the AI agent if it needs to fill in any guesswork or make decisions.

40
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:37,120
But it's not a debate, it's kind of a plan that the agent is going to execute for you.

41
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,960
This is an example of a typical spec you might see.

42
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,520
So this has a what and a why.

43
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,560
So this is a bit like the PRD elements about, you know, why are we building this thing?

44
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:49,924
What are we trying to solve?

45
00:03:50,318 --> 00:03:54,000
But then more importantly, we also want to define the constraints about what we're building.

46
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,480
So AI agents or coding agents in general are quite eager about solving problems.

47
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:09,920
So they're going to try and implement the thing, they're going to try and get it working, but there may be certain requirements we want to enforce, like don't use external libraries or don't use certain design patterns.

48
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:11,920
Some things may be out of scope.

49
00:04:12,238 --> 00:04:18,160
So if you go back to the add authentication example, there are so many possible things you could add that would be in scope there.

50
00:04:18,350 --> 00:04:21,572
You want to really shrink down the work to specifically what you want.

51
00:04:22,702 --> 00:04:26,208
And then ultimately we want to break down the work into a number of discrete tasks.

52
00:04:26,288 --> 00:04:32,900
So task one, what to build, which files to touch, and then how are we going to verify that that task is complete?

53
00:04:33,294 --> 00:04:37,088
So this is the same way we would break down tasks in an engineering team.

54
00:04:37,248 --> 00:04:42,928
We would break down the work into a number of tasks, and then we would give those tasks to engineers to work on.

55
00:04:43,246 --> 00:04:48,928
After the engineers are complete with a task, they would typically test the work, they would put it up for a code review.

56
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:51,088
And it's kind of the same cycle that we're doing here.

57
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:54,724
So here's a high-level view of how you might work with a spec-driven workflow.

58
00:04:55,022 --> 00:04:56,208
You generate a spec.

59
00:04:56,288 --> 00:05:03,844
If you've got a large enough bit of work that needs to be broken down over multiple tasks, maybe you're going to work on this over multiple coding sessions.

60
00:05:04,942 --> 00:05:06,848
You generate a spec using a meta prompt.

61
00:05:07,008 --> 00:05:09,568
So we describe to the AI agent what we want to build.

62
00:05:09,742 --> 00:05:11,808
The agent will write an initial spec.

63
00:05:11,982 --> 00:05:13,488
And then we're going to review that spec.

64
00:05:13,568 --> 00:05:17,604
We're going to iterate on it, make sure that all of the details in that spec are correct.

65
00:05:17,902 --> 00:05:21,540
Make sure the agent isn't having to guess about the implementation details.

66
00:05:21,934 --> 00:05:25,568
Then we break the work down into a number of tasks within the spec.

67
00:05:25,728 --> 00:05:27,428
The agent can help you do that as well.

68
00:05:27,886 --> 00:05:30,980
And then we want to run each of those tasks independently.

69
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:33,888
Then we're going to review the work, we're going to iterate, and we're going to commit.

70
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:37,988
And this is nice because we can gradually work through a task step by step.

71
00:05:38,414 --> 00:05:40,580
And this is the way we've always built software.

72
00:05:41,486 --> 00:05:46,116
You don't start by giving a software engineer an entire feature doc to go and build.

73
00:05:46,382 --> 00:05:52,128
You would typically give them a single task to work on and they would incrementally work through a problem step by step.

74
00:05:52,526 --> 00:05:54,768
So let's take a quick look at a specification example.

75
00:05:54,848 --> 00:05:59,780
So this is a hypothetical specification for adding JWT authentication to your app.

76
00:06:00,302 --> 00:06:01,648
So we start with why.

77
00:06:01,728 --> 00:06:04,132
So users currently share a single account.

78
00:06:04,494 --> 00:06:09,700
We need individual accounts to track usage per user for billing and to enable personal settings.

79
00:06:10,126 --> 00:06:11,888
Then we're defining what we want to do.

80
00:06:11,968 --> 00:06:16,288
So we want to make sure that users can register, login, and refresh tokens.

81
00:06:16,622 --> 00:06:22,244
We want JWT-based Auth with one hour access tokens and seven day refresh tokens.

82
00:06:22,798 --> 00:06:27,248
And you can see here already we're adding quite a lot of detail that is really important to the implementation.

83
00:06:27,488 --> 00:06:32,164
So one hour access tokens, we want to have seven day refresh tokens.

84
00:06:32,494 --> 00:06:40,484
This is the kind of detail that would get missed if you were just allowing an AI agent to go and execute a slightly less defined task for you.

85
00:06:41,422 --> 00:06:44,368
We're also being clear about how we want to structure the API.

86
00:06:44,654 --> 00:06:51,008
So when an AI agent is now given this specification, it's completely clear and non-ambiguous about what they need to build.

87
00:06:51,088 --> 00:06:53,156
So they're not having to guess about what you want.

88
00:06:53,934 --> 00:06:55,808
We're also defining some constraints.

89
00:06:56,048 --> 00:07:02,308
So using specific libraries to store the user data in Postgres via Prisma.

90
00:07:02,702 --> 00:07:05,796
And then we're also declaring about how to handle errors.

91
00:07:06,126 --> 00:07:12,964
And then we're also saying that you must not add new dependencies and you must not store tokens in the database.

92
00:07:13,422 --> 00:07:21,284
And then we're also talking about things that are out of scope, which an AI agent may otherwise go and implement because, again, they're very eager to build.

93
00:07:22,542 --> 00:07:25,488
And then what we do is we break the work down into a number of tasks.

94
00:07:25,568 --> 00:07:29,220
So you can see this is how a senior engineer might break down any bit of work.

95
00:07:29,582 --> 00:07:37,188
We start by adding a user model, and we can implement that task, and then we can verify it with some tests and we can review the code.

96
00:07:37,582 --> 00:07:40,688
We can commit that change, and then we can move on to the next task.

97
00:07:40,848 --> 00:07:48,528
So then we want to add the endpoints, we want to create the login endpoints, we create the refresh endpoints, and so on and so on.

98
00:07:48,608 --> 00:07:51,008
We go through the whole feature until we're done.

99
00:07:51,182 --> 00:08:00,448
And then afterwards, once we've finished all of the individual features, we may want to add some additional tests just to test the end-to-end flow and ensure everything works correctly.

100
00:08:00,814 --> 00:08:04,848
So as you can see, there's a lot of detail here for an AI agent to take.

101
00:08:05,038 --> 00:08:09,568
And so when the agent is going to implement the work, it's very clear about what it needs to do.

102
00:08:09,774 --> 00:08:15,648
It's worth mentioning that as you go through the specification, you may find that you implement task one.

103
00:08:15,728 --> 00:08:18,608
And when you review the code, you may see that something is wrong with the code.

104
00:08:18,688 --> 00:08:19,844
You may want to change something.

105
00:08:20,174 --> 00:08:22,368
You can always go back and change the spec.

106
00:08:22,528 --> 00:08:25,924
This isn't like a waterfall design where you have to know everything up front.

107
00:08:26,286 --> 00:08:29,444
You just want to give enough detail to the agent about what it needs to do.

108
00:08:29,806 --> 00:08:35,328
You can refine the spec, you can go and execute with an agent, see that there's something wrong, and then you can come back and correct it.

109
00:08:35,488 --> 00:08:36,288
That's totally fine.

110
00:08:36,448 --> 00:08:40,164
Here's another example where we're adding hybrid search with PG vector.

111
00:08:40,368 --> 00:08:41,808
This is a complicated feature.

112
00:08:41,888 --> 00:08:43,568
There's a lot of details here.

113
00:08:43,854 --> 00:08:52,132
And if you were to give this to an AI agent or if you were to vibe code this kind of thing, you know, you just kind of allow the agent to build it without you giving any input.

114
00:08:52,878 --> 00:08:55,600
There are so many things that the agent would probably do wrong.

115
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:58,000
So there are so many things to think about.

116
