233
00:16:07,616 --> 00:16:12,816
So one of the things you might be wondering is should I use a framework to implement spec-driven development?

234
00:16:12,896 --> 00:16:14,656
There are a lot of tools that are popular.

235
00:16:14,896 --> 00:16:16,336
Open spec is one of them.

236
00:16:16,416 --> 00:16:18,496
This is a relatively lightweight framework.

237
00:16:18,656 --> 00:16:23,044
You install a CLI, it will generate some of these files for you.

238
00:16:23,726 --> 00:16:26,416
Another popular tool is a GitHub spec kit.

239
00:16:27,630 --> 00:16:29,216
I found this pretty heavyweight.

240
00:16:29,296 --> 00:16:31,376
It generates tons of files.

241
00:16:31,758 --> 00:16:35,856
So to take you through the workflow with Kiro, you start with a requirements phase.

242
00:16:35,936 --> 00:16:40,256
You define user stories and acceptance criteria, then you go to a technical design.

243
00:16:40,336 --> 00:16:46,896
So again, this is mapping to the product requirements doc, technical design, and then the implementation tasks.

244
00:16:47,136 --> 00:16:53,536
You break this down into a number of discrete trackable tasks, and then you do the execution phase where you iterate through all of the tasks.

245
00:16:53,616 --> 00:16:55,876
You hand those over to an agent to implement.

246
00:16:56,334 --> 00:16:58,692
I found a lot of this stuff was overkill.

247
00:16:59,726 --> 00:17:03,236
I don't think you need to define user stories in Markdown.

248
00:17:03,630 --> 00:17:11,584
I think those things should be done in the traditional way where you define a product requirements doc, which is somewhat separate to the agent execution.

249
00:17:11,664 --> 00:17:14,180
I felt like these were just adding a ton of complexity.

250
00:17:14,542 --> 00:17:17,824
I don't think product managers should necessarily be editing markdown files.

251
00:17:17,904 --> 00:17:19,556
I don't think that makes it much sense.

252
00:17:19,950 --> 00:17:30,304
And similar with the design phase, I feel like there's a lot more detail that needs to go into an architectural document It doesn't really make sense in my opinion, in a markdown file.

253
00:17:30,510 --> 00:17:39,024
So if you're building anything non-trivial with AI agents, this workflow prevents agents from going and implementing things you don't want.

254
00:17:39,184 --> 00:17:43,492
It gives you more control over what you're building and it's going to lead to higher quality code.

255
00:17:43,918 --> 00:17:57,584
Working in a more incremental way is definitely preferable to allowing an AI agent to go and implement 10,000 lines of code, then you having to review that at the end is very difficult to unpick things once you get further down the line.

256
00:17:57,664 --> 00:18:01,380
So working in a more incremental manner makes just a ton of sense.

257
00:18:02,062 --> 00:18:04,996
And if you think about spec-driven development, there's nothing new here.

258
00:18:05,326 --> 00:18:08,612
In software engineering teams, we've always worked in the same way.

259
00:18:08,864 --> 00:18:15,024
For any non-trivial bit of work, a product manager or someone will come up with the requirements for what we need to build.

260
00:18:15,184 --> 00:18:16,996
Typically, this would be in a PRD.

261
00:18:17,582 --> 00:18:20,996
This document is for humans to agree on what to build.

262
00:18:21,454 --> 00:18:29,504
Engineers would typically break the work down into a technical design doc, which includes architecture breakdowns, details about how the feature is going to work.

263
00:18:29,664 --> 00:18:32,740
This would typically be reviewed by a number of engineers.

264
00:18:33,134 --> 00:18:38,212
Then you break the work down into a number of discrete tasks and you hand those tasks off to individual developers.

265
00:18:39,022 --> 00:18:42,372
The only real difference here is we're handing the tasks off to agents.

266
00:18:42,798 --> 00:18:59,744
So if you find yourself working on anything non-trivial, writing a specification first, storing it on local disk as a persistent file, reviewing the file, editing it, making sure all of the tasks are correct, making sure the agent has enough information to implement correctly is just a really good approach.

267
00:18:59,824 --> 00:19:05,984
And working in a more incremental manner will just raise the quality of the code when you're working with these AI coding agents.

